DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     aawwkk scans each _f_i_l_e (or the standard input, if none is
     given) for lines matching patterns given in an aawwkk program,
     and performs the associated actions.  The program is given
     either as the argument _p_r_o_g or, with -ff, read from _p_r_o_g_f_i_l_e.

     aawwkk recognizes the following options:

     -yy   Match patterns without regard to upper or lower case,
          as ggrreepp does with its -yy option.

     -FF_c  Set the input field separator to the character _c
          (default: blank and tab).

     -ff _p_r_o_g_f_i_l_e
          Take the aawwkk program from _p_r_o_g_f_i_l_e instead of from the
          command line.

     An aawwkk program consists of a sequence of _p_a_t_t_e_r_n -_a_c_t_i_o_n
     pairs.  For each input record (normally, each line), every
     pattern is tested; the action associated with each pattern
     that matches is performed.  Either the pattern or the action
     may be omitted: a missing pattern matches every record, and
     a missing action prints the matched record.  An action is a
     sequence of statements, enclosed in braces, in a language
     similar to C, operating on fields of the current record
     (referred to as $11,  $22,  ...), built-in variables such as
     NNRR (current record number), FFNNRR (record number within the
     current file) and NNFF (number of fields), and user-defined
     variables.  The expression _k_e_y iinn _a_r_r_a_y tests for an element
     without creating it, and eexxiitt may be given a status.

